  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A uniform ball of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;is placed on the top of a sphere of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>r</mi></math>. It is given a slight push due to which it starts&nbsp;rolling down the sphere without slipping. The spin angular velocity of the ball when it breaks off from the sphere is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mfenced></msqrt></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math>&nbsp;is the acceleration due to gravity and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>q</mi></math>&nbsp;are the smallest integers. What is the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></math>?</p>\r"
  },
